00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:59,120
Simgi Yun. It's titled Coming Back to the Human A-Sequel to Why Do We Live.

19
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:02,640
And I'll be perfectly honest with you, getting through this material requires a bit of

20
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:05,040
philosophical stamina.

21
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:06,640
That's a good way to put it, stamina.

22
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:10,640
You have to be willing to look at the things you hold absolutely sacred

23
00:01:11,200 --> 00:01:13,680
and just watch them dissolve under a microscope.

24
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:15,280
Which is necessary, right?

25
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:18,640
Because of the premise the author established in their previous work,

26
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:22,320
we kind of have to start there to understand the stakes of this essay.

27
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:26,160
Right. The original argument was that the meaning of life isn't something you discover.

28
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:30,000
Like you don't just wander around the desert waiting to unearth a glowing box of meaning.

29
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,760
Like it's buried treasure or something.

30
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:34,960
Exactly. It's not a treasure. It's something you actively create.

31
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:38,560
Which, you know, sounds super liberating at first glance.

32
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:41,600
It shifts the burden off the universe and onto you.

33
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:44,960
You don't have to beg the cosmos for your purpose. You just build it yourself.

34
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:50,400
But taking that idea seriously, like really seriously opens up a pretty massive sinkhole.

35
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:55,600
Yeah, a terrifying one. Because if the universe isn't handing out meaning,

36
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:57,120
and it's entirely up to us,

37
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:00,160
what actually deserves to be honored.

38
00:02:00,160 --> 00:02:02,400
That is the ultimate dilemma right there.

39
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,880
If you are the sole architect of your values,

40
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:09,200
and there is no like universal blueprint to check your work against,

41
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:10,640
what justifies anything?

42
00:02:10,640 --> 00:02:13,120
Why build a hospital instead of a casino, right?

43
00:02:13,120 --> 00:02:16,320
Exactly. Why pour your heart into raising children instead of just

44
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:18,560
staring blankly at a wall for 80 years?

45
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,560
Logically, it's one actually better than the other.

46
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:25,360
So today, we're going to use cold razor sharp logic to dismantle all of it.

47
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:28,000
We're going to strip away the romance of human connection,

48
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:30,480
tear down the whole concept of human greatness,

49
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:34,080
and just look directly into the absolute void of meaninglessness.

50
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:35,120
It sounds bleak, I know.

51
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:38,800
But, and this is why this deep dive is so vital,

52
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:40,400
we aren't staying in the void.

53
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:45,120
We're going to find a wildly unexpected, deeply profound reason to care about

54
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:46,800
this messy human experience anyway.

55
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:49,520
We just have to survive the dismantling first.

56
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:52,160
And before we even pick up the tools to do that,

57
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:55,040
the author hits us with a massive structural roadblock.

58
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:56,400
Yeah, the meta problem.

59
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:56,720
Right.

60
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:00,160
The very tool we are using right now,

61
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:02,960
the English language, the actual words forming in your mind,

62
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:04,080
as you listen to us,

63
00:03:04,080 --> 00:03:09,440
might be fundamentally incapable of grasping the truth we're hunting for here.

64
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:11,520
The language trap, I love this part,

65
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:14,480
because it's like trying to catch water with a fishnet.

66
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:16,800
You can see the water, you can describe the water,

67
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:19,040
but the net itself just can't hold it.

68
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:21,360
The author grounds this whole problem

69
00:03:21,360 --> 00:03:23,360
by bringing in Ludwig Wittgenstein.

70
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:26,320
Oh, man, Wittgenstein.

71
00:03:26,320 --> 00:03:30,080
Arguably the most intense philosopher of language from the 20th century.

72
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:32,640
He wrote this notoriously dense book that tracked at us,

73
00:03:32,640 --> 00:03:35,040
which is basically impossible to read casually.

74
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:35,760
Oh, completely.

75
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:37,360
And he concluded it with this famous line,

76
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:39,680
where of one cannot speak,

77
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:41,520
there of one must be silent.

78
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:42,960
So a lot of thinkers read that line,

79
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:47,120
and they assume Wittgenstein reached the summit of human thought.

80
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,440
They think he arrived at the same mystical,

81
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:52,400
transcendent silence that Eastern contemplative traditions

82
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:53,760
have been teaching for millennia.

83
00:03:53,760 --> 00:03:56,240
But the author makes a brilliant distinction here.

84
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:59,120
Wittgenstein didn't actually cross into the silence.

85
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:01,120
He just drew a map of the noise.

86
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:03,440
He mapped the boundary from the inside.

87
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:04,640
Exactly.

88
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:09,520
He used logic to push language to its absolute breaking point,

89
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:13,280
drew a circle around everything we can rationally talk about,

90
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:14,800
and basically said,

91
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:17,200
OK, outside this circle is the unspeakable.

92
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:19,120
Don't even try to go there.

93
00:04:19,120 --> 00:04:22,160
He's like a cartographer drawing dragons at the edge of the map,

94
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:25,440
but he never actually sails the ship into those uncharted waters.

95
00:04:25,440 --> 00:04:28,960
He stays totally safe inside the realm of logic.

96
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:32,560
He's essentially saying that language is a logical picture of facts.

97
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:36,720
And since the meaning of the world lies outside the facts of the world,

98
00:04:36,720 --> 00:04:38,080
language just can't touch it.

99
00:04:38,080 --> 00:04:39,680
It's a brilliant diagnosis.

100
00:04:39,680 --> 00:04:42,000
It is, but it's still just a diagnosis.

101
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:43,840
The whole climbing of a ladder,

102
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:46,880
then throwing away the ladder that Wittgenstein talks about,

103
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:49,520
it remains a purely intellectual exercise.

104
00:04:49,520 --> 00:04:52,160
Right, to actually experience what lies beyond that boundary,

105
00:04:52,160 --> 00:04:54,800
we have to look at the philosophical escalation that happens

106
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:57,360
when thinkers try to break out of the map entirely.

107
00:04:57,360 --> 00:04:58,800
Unless trace that escalation,

108
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,960
because the author lays it up beautifully,

109
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:03,600
we start with Wittgenstein pointing at the boundary.

110
00:05:03,600 --> 00:05:05,760
The next massive leap is Edmund Hussle.

111
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:07,200
The father of phenomenology.

112
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,200
Right. Hussle isn't just drawing a map.

113
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:13,040
He's basically inventing a new way to use your brain.

114
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:15,360
He develops this concept called the epique,

115
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:19,120
which is usually translated as bracketing the natural attitude, right?

116
00:05:19,120 --> 00:05:22,240
Yeah, bracketing, which I know sounds like an accounting term,

117
00:05:22,240 --> 00:05:24,080
but let's actually walk through what that feels like.

118
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:26,400
Yeah, please, because it's a weird concept.

119
00:05:26,400 --> 00:05:28,080
So normally you move through the world,

120
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:30,480
taking reality totally of a granted.

121
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,840
You look at, say, a coffee cup on your desk,

122
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:36,800
and your brain instantly labels it.

123
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:38,880
My favorite ceramic cup.

124
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:40,320
It holds hot liquid.

125
00:05:40,320 --> 00:05:42,400
It exists subjectively in space.

126
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:46,240
You are basically drowning in assumptions and histories and categories.

127
00:05:46,240 --> 00:05:47,280
You're not seeing the cup.

128
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:49,040
You're seeing the concept of a cup.

129
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:49,920
Exactly.

130
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:53,680
Hussle's epique is the practice of mentally putting all those concepts.

131
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:55,200
The ceramic part, the utility,

132
00:05:55,200 --> 00:05:59,120
even the belief that the cup exists independently of you into brackets.

133
00:05:59,120 --> 00:06:00,880
You suspend your judgment entirely.

134
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:02,960
Like pausing the mental software.

135
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:03,920
Yes.

136
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:06,800
You stop analyzing what the object is in the real world.

137
00:06:07,360 --> 00:06:10,240
And you force your attention onto the pure,

138
00:06:10,240 --> 00:06:14,080
raw phenomenon of the object appearing in your consciousness.

139
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:16,080
It's a massive mental shift.

140
00:06:16,080 --> 00:06:19,360
You're no longer playing Vickinstein's logic game at all.

141
00:06:19,360 --> 00:06:24,000
You're doing something closer to like an intense,

142
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,760
hyper-focused meditation.

143
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:28,080
You are training a specific mode of attention

144
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:29,600
to bypass the labels.

145
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:32,000
And that leads directly to the next escalation,

146
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,120
which is Martin Heidegger,

147
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:35,040
who is actually Hussle's student.

148
00:06:35,040 --> 00:06:36,560
Heidegger takes this practice

149
00:06:36,560 --> 00:06:39,600
and applies it to the very nature of existence itself.

150
00:06:39,600 --> 00:06:41,760
He introduces concepts like Alaskan height,

151
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:44,880
which translates roughly to letting B or releasement.

152
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:48,720
Heidegger wanted to dynamite the entire history of Western philosophy, didn't he?

153
00:06:48,720 --> 00:06:51,280
Because he thought it was obsessed with categorizing things

154
00:06:51,280 --> 00:06:53,440
as objects to be studied by subjects.

155
00:06:53,440 --> 00:06:54,480
He really did.

156
00:06:54,480 --> 00:06:57,440
He wanted to get before the split between the subject and the object.

157
00:06:57,440 --> 00:06:59,440
Okay, so think about the moments in your life

158
00:06:59,440 --> 00:07:01,920
when you are completely immersed in an activity.

159
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:06,000
Maybe you're playing a piano or surfing or coding in a deep flow state.

160
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,760
Flow state is the perfect example.

161
00:07:07,760 --> 00:07:11,280
In those moments, there is no you analyzing the piano.

162
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:13,120
The boundary just vanishes.

163
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:14,960
There is just the music happening.

164
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:16,480
Heidegger believed that this state

165
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,000
getting behind that artificial wall

166
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,080
between observer and observed

167
00:07:20,080 --> 00:07:24,080
was the only way to glimpse being with a capital B.

168
00:07:24,080 --> 00:07:26,160
It's really no surprise that in his later years,

169
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:29,040
his work started to deeply echo East Asian thought,

170
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:31,200
especially Taoist philosophy.

171
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:34,880
Which brings us to the absolute peak of this progression in the essay.

172
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:37,840
We go from Vickinstein mapping the limits of words

173
00:07:37,840 --> 00:07:41,920
to hostile suspending labels to Heidegger dissolving the observer

174
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:44,000
and finally to the Buddha.

175
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:47,760
And the author argues that the Buddha wasn't just theorizing about any of this.

176
00:07:47,760 --> 00:07:50,480
The Buddha was pure unadulterated practice.

177
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:52,960
There is this stunning historical detail

178
00:07:52,960 --> 00:07:54,960
the author includes to illustrate this.

179
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:57,440
After wandering and teaching for 45 years,

180
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:00,160
delivering thousands of discourses to his followers,

181
