the Buddha is said to have declared on his deathbed.

182
00:08:03,040 --> 00:08:04,960
I have not said a single word.

183
00:08:04,960 --> 00:08:05,920
Which is wild.

184
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:09,360
Right. If you look at that through Vickinstein's purely logical lens,

185
00:08:09,360 --> 00:08:11,440
it's nonsense. It's a blatant contradiction.

186
00:08:11,440 --> 00:08:13,600
The guy talked for four and a half decades.

187
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:16,080
But if you understand the escalation we just mapped out,

188
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:19,280
it is the most coherent thing he could possibly say.

189
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,840
Those 45 years of speaking were never meant to be objective truths.

190
00:08:24,720 --> 00:08:27,360
They were, in the classic ven metaphor,

191
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:29,280
merely fingers pointing at the moon.

192
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:31,680
The words are the finger.

193
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:34,240
The direct experience of reality is the moon.

194
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:37,600
If you stare at the finger, you miss the sky completely.

195
00:08:37,600 --> 00:08:40,400
The author brings in the diamond sutra to drive this home.

196
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:43,440
It's perhaps the most famous text in Mahayana Buddhism.

197
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:47,680
And the entire sutra is a relentless assault on language using language.

198
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:50,000
Exactly. It repeats over and over

199
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:53,520
that all forms or illusions that the Buddha has no fixed teaching,

200
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:57,280
that saying you have achieved enlightenment actually proves you haven't.

201
00:08:57,280 --> 00:09:01,120
It operates entirely on the principle of direct seeing the Chinese character Guan.

202
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:05,040
Guan. It's perception without the filter of vocabulary.

203
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:08,880
So to bring this back to you, the listener, we have a problem here today.

204
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:10,880
We have this time together to do a deep dive.

205
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:15,360
And the only tool we have to connect with you is the very language we just proved is flawed.

206
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:16,880
We are in the language trap.

207
00:09:17,520 --> 00:09:20,160
And the author acknowledges this trap openly in the text.

208
00:09:20,160 --> 00:09:22,720
They write, I will use language in what follows.

209
00:09:22,720 --> 00:09:23,920
I have no choice.

210
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:25,920
We are in the exact same boat.

211
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:30,000
We have to use the scaffolding of words to build these concepts for you.

212
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,000
But the critical takeaway for you listening is how you process what you're hearing.

213
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:39,680
The words we're using today are just the exhaust fumes of the actual realization.

214
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:41,840
Oh, I like that. The exhaust fumes.

215
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:45,360
Right. They are the footprints in the dirt, not the creature that left them.

216
00:09:45,360 --> 00:09:49,120
So as we move into the actual dismantling of human meaning,

217
00:09:49,120 --> 00:09:55,360
try to look past our syntax, look at the vast, terrifying, empty space the words are pointing toward.

218
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:59,760
With that caveat in place, we can pick up the actual instrument the author uses

219
00:09:59,760 --> 00:10:01,920
to dissect human experience.

220
00:10:01,920 --> 00:10:05,200
Because if words are flawed, what internal mechanism do we rely on?

221
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:07,360
The author chooses rational thinking.

222
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:11,440
But they immediately strict away all the romantic mythology we usually attach to it.

223
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:14,720
This was a major wake-up call for me reading the text.

224
00:10:14,720 --> 00:10:19,200
We have this deep-seated, incredibly flattering self-image in Western culture.

225
00:10:19,840 --> 00:10:24,880
We think of ourselves as these autonomous, free thinking captains of our own destiny.

226
00:10:24,880 --> 00:10:28,640
We really believe our rationality makes us independent from the universe.

227
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:31,600
The author takes a sledgehammer to that illusion.

228
00:10:31,600 --> 00:10:34,080
Strict autonomy simply does not exist.

229
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:34,720
It's a fiction.

230
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:40,800
You are an organism shaped by a relentless barrage of external forces,

231
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:46,240
your environment, your genetic inheritance, the specific grammatical structure of your native language.

232
00:10:46,240 --> 00:10:48,960
The unspoken belief systems of your zip code.

233
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:53,040
Yes. All of these things have been stamped on your nervous system without your consent.

234
00:10:53,040 --> 00:10:56,080
It really makes you question every original thought you've ever had.

235
00:10:56,080 --> 00:10:59,600
Like, when I think I'm making a deeply personal independent choice,

236
00:10:59,600 --> 00:11:04,880
I'm usually just executing a complex software program written by my culture and my biology.

237
00:11:04,880 --> 00:11:07,200
Your originality is largely a mirage.

238
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:09,680
But wait, this is where I push back when I was reading.

239
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:15,200
If my free will is an illusion, and I'm basically just a meat computer running cultural software,

