00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:42,240
We use it because it's empirically the best tool for achieving very specific outcomes.

249
00:11:42,240 --> 00:11:44,640
They lay out two concrete criteria.

250
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:49,280
We use reason because it maximizes our ability to make our own lives unfold

251
00:11:49,280 --> 00:11:50,960
in ways we find desirable.

252
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:56,080
And secondly, it maximizes our ability to ensure our actions do good for humanity

253
00:11:56,080 --> 00:11:57,600
and the broader universe.

254
00:11:57,600 --> 00:11:59,040
Notice what's missing there, though.

255
00:11:59,040 --> 00:12:01,680
The author isn't claiming that making your life desirable

256
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:04,160
is objectively true with a capital T.

257
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:05,120
No, not at all.

258
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:07,040
It's just saying, look, since you're here,

259
00:12:07,040 --> 00:12:10,480
and since you clearly prefer feeling good over feeling terrible,

260
00:12:10,480 --> 00:12:13,360
and preferring a functioning society over a collapsed one,

261
00:12:13,840 --> 00:12:17,200
rationality is the sharpest tool in the shed to get you there.

262
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:19,760
The alternative is relying on emotional reasoning.

263
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:22,960
And the author issues a severe warning about that.

264
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:28,640
Relying on your emotions to decide what is honorable or meaningful is a total trap.

265
00:12:28,640 --> 00:12:32,720
Because your emotions are the very things that have been programmed by your environment.

266
00:12:32,720 --> 00:12:33,360
Exactly.

267
00:12:33,360 --> 00:12:36,320
If you rely on a gut feeling to tell you what's noble,

268
00:12:36,320 --> 00:12:39,680
your gut is just going to regurgitate whatever society fed it.

269
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:43,360
Right. Like if you're born into a hyper-militaristic society,

270
00:12:43,360 --> 00:12:46,880
your emotions will naturally swell with pride at the thought of dying

271
00:12:46,880 --> 00:12:48,560
in a completely pointless war.

272
00:12:48,560 --> 00:12:50,800
Or if you're born into modern hustle culture,

273
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:53,440
your emotions will convince you that sacrificing your physical health

274
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:56,080
for a corporate promotion is the highest form of honor.

275
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:59,520
The author summarizes this with a chilling phrase.

276
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:05,280
Relying purely on inherited emotional responses is being thought by one's environment.

277
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:06,960
Being thought by one's environment.

278
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:09,280
You aren't generating thoughts.

279
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:12,240
The environment is using your brain to generate its thoughts.

280
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:15,040
Your mind is just a colony for cultural viruses.

281
00:13:15,040 --> 00:13:16,880
So rationality is our scalpel.

282
00:13:17,440 --> 00:13:21,120
It's the only instrument sharp enough to cut through the dense layers

283
00:13:21,120 --> 00:13:23,840
of inherited programming and sever the puppet strings.

284
00:13:23,840 --> 00:13:25,760
And that brings us to the core of the essay.

285
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:26,640
We have our scalpel.

286
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:31,360
The author asks us to take this tool of pragmatic rationality

287
00:13:31,360 --> 00:13:34,400
and apply it ruthlessly to everything we consider sacred,

288
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:37,120
beautiful, and honorable in the human experience.

289
00:13:37,120 --> 00:13:38,640
We are stepping into the dissolution.

290
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:39,920
We are falling into the void.

291
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:44,560
And the author goes straight for the throat by dissecting romantic love.

292
00:13:44,640 --> 00:13:45,680
Yeah, they don't hold back.

293
00:13:45,680 --> 00:13:47,920
Think about the pedestal we put romantic love on.

294
00:13:48,560 --> 00:13:51,920
Thousands of years of poetry, Shakespeare, pop music.

295
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:53,520
Romeo and Juliet, dying for each other,

296
00:13:53,520 --> 00:13:56,640
is basically the ultimate cultural symbol of nobility.

297
00:13:56,640 --> 00:14:00,560
Love is supposedly this cosmic force that transcends time and space.

298
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:02,560
But let's apply this scalpel.

299
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:02,880
Yeah.

300
00:14:02,880 --> 00:14:04,720
What happens when you look at romantic love

301
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:07,040
through the cold lens of rational analysis?

302
00:14:07,040 --> 00:14:08,960
The poetry just evaporates.

303
00:14:08,960 --> 00:14:11,120
And you were left with evolutionary biology.

304
00:14:11,120 --> 00:14:14,320
It's just neurochemistry and DNA trying to propagate itself.

305
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:19,040
The intense gravitational pull you feel toward another human being

306
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:21,440
is a highly optimized survival mechanism.

307
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:25,440
We feel romantic love because the specific DNA sequences

308
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:27,920
that predisposed our early ancestors to bond,

309
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:30,160
stay together and protect their offspring

310
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:34,000
were the sequences that survived the brutal filter of natural selection.

311
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:35,760
The ancestors who didn't fall in love

312
00:14:35,760 --> 00:14:38,560
who felt no biological urge to protect their mates,

313
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:40,880
they simply didn't pass on their genes.

314
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:44,160
Their indifference was bred out of the gene pool entirely.

315
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:46,400
So that mystical feeling of two souls

316
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:48,080
finding each other in the dark,

317
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:51,200
it's a blind, unthinking optimization process.

318
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:53,680
Juliet is just a biological vessel

319
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:56,800
for Romeo's genetic material to replicate and vice versa.

320
00:14:56,800 --> 00:14:59,920
Which is such a brutally unromantic thing to say out loud.

321
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:01,200
Imagine telling your partner,

322
00:15:01,200 --> 00:15:04,720
hey, my DNA just really wants to replicate with yours.

323
00:15:04,720 --> 00:15:05,760
I wouldn't recommend it.

324
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:07,840
But the scalpel doesn't stop at romance.

325
00:15:07,840 --> 00:15:10,720
It cuts through the entire spectrum of human emotion.

326
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:14,400
Joy, sorrow, righteous anger, empathy,

327
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:16,480
even the feeling of profound inspiration.

328
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:17,760
That one hurts the most, I think.

329
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:22,160
The idea that feeling inspired to create art or build something new

330
00:15:22,160 --> 00:15:23,840
isn't a touch of the divine,

331
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:25,520
but just another survival tool.

332
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,960
Feelings of ambition or inspiration drive organisms

333
00:15:28,960 --> 00:15:31,200
to overcome environmental obstacles,

334
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:34,080
secure more resources and elevate their status

335
00:15:34,080 --> 00:15:35,600
within a social hierarchy.

336
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:39,680
And all of that drastically improves the odds of survival and reproduction.

337
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:43,600
Every emotion, no matter how sacred it feels from the inside,

338
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:46,880
is just a nervous system configuration designed

339
00:15:46,880 --> 00:15:48,640
to navigate a physical environment.

340
00:15:48,640 --> 00:15:51,600
The universe doesn't care if you feel joy or despair.

341
00:15:51,600 --> 00:15:54,240
Your brain just uses those emotional states

342
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:56,000
as internal carrots and sticks.

343
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:59,840
There is absolutely no inherent cosmic honor in any of it.

344
00:15:59,840 --> 00:16:02,240
Let's turn the scalpel toward another massive pillar

345
00:16:02,240 --> 00:16:05,360
of human meaning, greatness and ambition.

346
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:07,520
We build monuments to historical figures.

347
00:16:07,520 --> 00:16:09,040
We obsess over visionaries.

348
00:16:09,120 --> 00:16:11,680
The author points to figures like tech CEO Jensen Huang

349
00:16:11,680 --> 00:16:12,640
or Albert Einstein.

350
00:16:13,200 --> 00:16:16,320
We treat their achievements driving technological innovation,

351
00:16:16,320 --> 00:16:17,760
unraveling the laws of physics

352
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:20,000
as the absolute pinnacle of human worth.

353
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,040
But why?

354
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:24,720
Why is a human building a supercomputer inherently more valuable

355
00:16:24,720 --> 00:16:26,640
or honorable than a beaver building a dam?

356
00:16:27,360 --> 00:16:30,240
Or, frankly, more honorable than the intricate

357
00:16:30,240 --> 00:16:33,200
mathematical perfection of a hurricane forming over the ocean?

358
00:16:33,200 --> 00:16:35,280
When you trace the logic all the way down,

359
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,960
you find that our worship of human greatness

360
00:16:38,960 --> 00:16:40,800
is a completely closed loop.

361
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:44,240
We honor human achievements because we are humans,

362
00:16:44,240 --> 00:16:46,720
operating inside a human-centric culture

363
00:16:46,720 --> 00:16:49,600
that invented the concept of achievement in the first place.

364
00:16:49,600 --> 00:16:51,760
We built the game, wrote the rules,

365
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:53,040
designed the scoreboard,

366
00:16:53,040 --> 00:16:56,000
and now we bow down to the people who rack up the most points.

367
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:56,960
It's totally circular.

368
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:59,360
We honor this because this is what our species does.

369
00:16:59,360 --> 00:17:01,920
There's no foundation beneath the four boards,

370
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:04,240
which naturally leads people to try and zoom out, right?

371
00:17:04,320 --> 00:17:05,840
When individual meaning fails,

372
00:17:05,840 --> 00:17:07,440
we usually try to attach ourselves

373
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:09,680
to a massive collective philosophy.

374
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:10,800
The isms.

375
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:13,440
Humanism is the dominant one in the secular world.

376
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:15,040
The belief that the ultimate good

377
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:17,280
is the flourishing and progress of the human race.

378
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:18,560
It sounds unassailable.

379
00:17:18,560 --> 00:17:21,920
I mean, who wants to argue against the flourishing of humanity?

380
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:24,240
But the scalpel demands we ask.

381
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:25,520
Why humanism?

382
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:28,000
If you are relying purely on rationality,

383
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,160
you cannot logically prove that humanism

384
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:31,680
is the correct viewpoint.

385
00:17:31,680 --> 00:17:33,120
Why not animalism,

386
00:17:33,120 --> 00:17:37,120
where we value the flourishing of all sentient life equally,

387
00:17:37,120 --> 00:17:38,640
which would mean human convenience

388
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:41,520
must be radically sacrificed for the comfort of livestock,

389
00:17:41,520 --> 00:17:43,760
or take it even further to what the author calls,

390
00:17:43,760 --> 00:17:45,040
Earthism,

391
00:17:45,040 --> 00:17:48,080
valuing the flourishing of the entire planetary ecosystem

392
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:49,760
above any single species.

393
00:17:49,760 --> 00:17:51,520
If you adopt Earthism,

394
00:17:51,520 --> 00:17:54,000
humanism suddenly looks like a massive threat.

395
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:57,040
The author brings in a phenomenal pop culture reference here,

396
00:17:57,040 --> 00:17:58,560
Agent Smith from The Matrix.

397
00:17:58,560 --> 00:17:59,840
No, I love this part.

398
00:17:59,840 --> 00:18:02,160
Agent Smith famously interrogates Morpheus

399
00:18:02,160 --> 00:18:04,400
and describes humanity not as a mammal,

400
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:05,760
but as a virus,

401
00:18:05,760 --> 00:18:06,800
a cancer of the planet

402
00:18:06,800 --> 00:18:08,560
that endlessly consumes resources

403
00:18:08,560 --> 00:18:09,920
until the host is dead.

404
00:18:09,920 --> 00:18:11,760
From the perspective of Earthism,

405
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:12,800
wiping out humanity

406
00:18:12,800 --> 00:18:15,520
might be the most honorable, rational course of action.

407
00:18:15,520 --> 00:18:17,040
Or what about alienism?

408
00:18:17,040 --> 00:18:18,720
The idea that we should prioritize

409
00:18:18,720 --> 00:18:20,480
the flourishing of some hyper-advanced

410
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:21,840
intergalactic intelligence

411
00:18:21,840 --> 00:18:23,680
that views us the way we view ants.

412
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:25,280
The underlying philosophical point

413
00:18:25,280 --> 00:18:27,040
is that none of these elizisms

414
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:28,800
can be grounded in anything deeper

415
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:30,560
than an arbitrary starting premise.

416
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:32,960
Rationality is a mechanism

417
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:35,520
for moving from premise A to conclusion B.

418
00:18:35,520 --> 00:18:37,200
It cannot prove premise A.

419
00:18:37,200 --> 00:18:38,560
You have to blindly assume

420
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:40,560
that human life is the most valuable thing

421
00:18:40,560 --> 00:18:42,480
in order to argue for humanism.

422
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:43,760
You can't use logic

423
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:45,120
to prove the very premise

424
00:18:45,120 --> 00:18:46,720
that logic requires to function.

425
00:18:46,720 --> 00:18:48,640
And this is the moment we hit the bottom.

426
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:50,640
We have deconstructed love,

427
00:18:50,640 --> 00:18:51,840
emotion,

428
00:18:51,840 --> 00:18:52,960
greatness,

429
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:55,040
and every grand philosophy.

430
00:18:55,040 --> 00:18:56,800
Every single standpoint dissolves

431
00:18:56,800 --> 00:18:58,160
under the pressure of the scalpel.

432
00:18:58,160 --> 00:18:59,600
The author states the reality

433
00:18:59,600 --> 00:19:02,160
of the situation with brutal clarity.

434
00:19:02,160 --> 00:19:03,520
There is, in this sense,

435
00:19:03,520 --> 00:19:05,920
nothing honorable to do in this world period.

436
00:19:05,920 --> 00:19:06,720
I want to pause here

437
00:19:06,720 --> 00:19:08,880
because it is so easy to misinterpret this

438
00:19:08,880 --> 00:19:10,960
as just dark edgy nihilism.

439
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:11,280
Right.

440
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:13,440
It feels like we're just saying everything is trash.

441
00:19:13,440 --> 00:19:15,280
But the author leans on David Hume,

442
00:19:15,280 --> 00:19:17,280
the 18th century Scottish philosopher,

443
00:19:17,840 --> 00:19:19,840
and his famous isot distinction

444
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:21,440
to clarify this void.

445
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:23,520
Hume's insight is crucial here.

446
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:25,760
He pointed out that you cannot deduce

447
00:19:25,760 --> 00:19:26,800
an ought,

448
00:19:26,800 --> 00:19:28,080
a moral imperative

449
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:29,680
about how things should be

450
00:19:29,680 --> 00:19:30,880
from an is,

451
00:19:30,880 --> 00:19:33,360
a factual observation about how things are.

452
00:19:33,360 --> 00:19:35,040
Just because we use the scalpel

453
00:19:35,040 --> 00:19:37,120
to discover that love is a biological trick

454
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:38,640
to ensure reproduction

455
00:19:38,640 --> 00:19:40,320
doesn't automatically mean love

456
00:19:40,320 --> 00:19:42,160
ought to be abandoned as worthless.

457
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:44,480
The fact that our values have a contingent,

458
00:19:44,480 --> 00:19:46,640
evolutionary, random origin

459
00:19:46,640 --> 00:19:49,120
doesn't automatically dictate their normative status.

460
00:19:49,120 --> 00:19:51,280
It doesn't mean they are inherently trash.

461
00:19:51,280 --> 00:19:53,840
The specific phrasing the author uses is vital.

462
00:19:54,560 --> 00:19:56,000
These things aren't meaningless.

463
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:57,840
They just lack a cosmic warrant.

464
00:19:57,840 --> 00:19:59,200
A cosmic warrant.

465
00:19:59,200 --> 00:20:00,480
I love that term.

466
00:20:00,480 --> 00:20:02,080
The universe didn't sign a certificate

467
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:03,520
guaranteeing that your ambition

468
00:20:03,520 --> 00:20:05,920
or your compassion has objective value.

469
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:08,480
There is no supreme judge keeping score.

470
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:09,520
The void is real,

471
00:20:09,520 --> 00:20:10,720
the universe is silent,

472
00:20:10,720 --> 00:20:12,000
and there is no safety net.

473
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:13,120
And if we stop here,

474
00:20:13,120 --> 00:20:14,320
we are stranded.

475
00:20:14,320 --> 00:20:15,680
We're completely paralyzed

476
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:17,360
and pure nihilism.

477
00:20:17,360 --> 00:20:18,880
But the author makes a fascinating

478
00:20:18,880 --> 00:20:20,640
self-correction regarding their own

479
00:20:20,640 --> 00:20:21,920
intellectual journey.

480
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:23,280
They realize that reason got them

481
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:24,320
to the bottom of the void,

482
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:26,320
but it fundamentally lacked the machinery

483
00:20:26,320 --> 00:20:27,200
to get them out.

484
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:29,040
If rationality is so powerful,

485
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:30,400
why can't it rebuild meaning?

486
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:33,360
Because of the nature of the tool itself,

487
00:20:33,360 --> 00:20:35,200
rationality is a scalpel.

488
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:36,720
It is an instrument of division,

489
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:37,920
of dissection.

490
00:20:37,920 --> 00:20:39,520
It cuts away disease tissue

491
00:20:39,520 --> 00:20:41,200
and severs illusions,

492
00:20:41,200 --> 00:20:43,920
but you cannot use a scalpel to grow a garden.

493
00:20:43,920 --> 00:20:46,320
You cannot deduce your way into a meaningful life.

494
00:20:46,320 --> 00:20:48,080
If you rely purely on logic,

495
00:20:48,080 --> 00:20:50,000
you just stand in an empty field,

496
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:51,360
holding a sharp knife,

497
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:53,280
surrounded by the shredded pieces

498
00:20:53,280 --> 00:20:54,640
of your cultural programming.

499
00:20:54,640 --> 00:20:57,280
Rationality cleared the plot of land perfectly,

500
00:20:57,280 --> 00:20:59,040
but it has no seeds to plant.

501
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:00,320
The return to living

502
00:21:00,320 --> 00:21:02,560
requires a completely different mechanism.

503
00:21:02,560 --> 00:21:03,920
We need a second engine.

504
00:21:03,920 --> 00:21:05,520
To define the second engine,

505
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:08,240
the author points to what we broadly call wisdom

506
00:21:08,240 --> 00:21:10,000
and draws on three distinct

507
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,080
philosophical traditions to explain it.

508
00:21:12,080 --> 00:21:14,800
First is Aristotle's concept of frenesis

509
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:16,160
or practical wisdom.

510
00:21:16,160 --> 00:21:18,720
This isn't book smarts or logical deduction.

511
00:21:18,720 --> 00:21:20,720
It's the lived intuitive knowledge

512
00:21:20,720 --> 00:21:22,400
of how to act in complex,

513
00:21:22,400 --> 00:21:24,240
mythy human situations

514
00:21:24,240 --> 00:21:26,000
where strict rules break down.

515
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,480
Second, they invoke Jean-Paul Sart

516
00:21:28,480 --> 00:21:30,560
and the existentialist tradition.

517
00:21:30,560 --> 00:21:32,240
The core of existentialism

518
00:21:32,240 --> 00:21:35,120
is that existence precedes essence.

519
00:21:35,120 --> 00:21:37,120
You exist first in the void

520
00:21:37,120 --> 00:21:39,280
and you must actively choose your essence.

521
00:21:39,280 --> 00:21:42,000
You don't discover your path through logic.

522
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,800
You forge it through bold, unjustified choice.

523
00:21:44,800 --> 00:21:47,360
And the third, which I think is just the most beautiful,

524
00:21:47,360 --> 00:21:49,680
comes back to Mahayana Buddhism.

525
00:21:49,680 --> 00:21:51,200
It's the idea of compassion,

526
00:21:51,360 --> 00:21:53,120
that arises spontaneously

527
00:21:53,120 --> 00:21:55,200
from the realization of emptiness calm.

528
00:21:55,200 --> 00:21:57,440
In the West, we usually equate emptiness

529
00:21:57,440 --> 00:21:58,800
with a cold dead vacuum.

530
00:21:59,760 --> 00:22:01,040
But in this tradition,

531
00:22:01,040 --> 00:22:02,400
truly realizing the void,

532
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:04,720
realizing that you and everyone around you

533
00:22:04,720 --> 00:22:08,400
lack any permanent, inherent self or cosmic armor.

534
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:09,760
Doesn't breed apathy.

535
00:22:09,760 --> 00:22:11,840
It breeds profound empathy.

536
00:22:11,840 --> 00:22:14,240
You see the sheer vulnerability of being alive

537
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:16,160
and compassion is the natural response

538
00:22:16,160 --> 00:22:18,720
of an awakened mind to that shared vulnerability.

539
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:20,000
The author gives us an analogy

540
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:21,840
that perfectly unifies all these concepts.

541
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:22,320
Yeah.

542
00:22:22,320 --> 00:22:23,520
Reason is the scalpel.

543
00:22:23,520 --> 00:22:25,840
Wisdom is the hand that knows when to put the scalpel down.

544
00:22:25,840 --> 00:22:27,840
It perfectly captures the tension.

545
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:30,720
I want you to think about this dynamic in your own life.

546
00:22:31,440 --> 00:22:32,960
We all know someone or maybe,

547
00:22:32,960 --> 00:22:33,840
you know, we've been this person

548
00:22:33,840 --> 00:22:35,360
who cannot put the scalpel down.

549
00:22:35,360 --> 00:22:37,200
They hyperanalyze everything.

550
00:22:37,200 --> 00:22:37,920
Everything.

551
00:22:37,920 --> 00:22:40,320
They dissect their romantic relationships,

552
00:22:40,320 --> 00:22:42,240
reading into every single text message

553
00:22:42,240 --> 00:22:43,600
and tone of voice,

554
00:22:43,600 --> 00:22:46,640
logically deconstructing their partners' motives

555
00:22:46,640 --> 00:22:48,720
until the relationship essentially

556
00:22:48,720 --> 00:22:50,560
bleeds out on the operating table.

557
00:22:50,560 --> 00:22:52,560
Or they analyze a career passion

558
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:54,000
until they logically prove it's

559
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,080
statistically unlikely to succeed

560
00:22:56,080 --> 00:22:57,360
and they lose all joy in it.

561
00:22:57,360 --> 00:23:00,000
The purely rational person stays in the void

562
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:01,760
shredding everything they touch.

563
00:23:01,760 --> 00:23:04,160
But the wise person uses the scalpel

564
00:23:04,160 --> 00:23:06,080
to cut away toxic illusions

565
00:23:06,080 --> 00:23:07,600
like the pressure to conform

566
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:09,920
to society's definition of success

567
00:23:09,920 --> 00:23:12,240
and then deliberately puts the blade away.

568
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:14,960
They consciously decide to experience the relationship.

569
00:23:15,040 --> 00:23:16,800
They choose to paint the canvas,

570
00:23:16,800 --> 00:23:18,640
knowing the cosmos doesn't care,

571
00:23:18,640 --> 00:23:19,920
but choosing to care anyway.

572
00:23:19,920 --> 00:23:22,160
Which brings us to the actual act of living,

573
00:23:22,160 --> 00:23:23,840
what the author calls the return.

574
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:26,160
With the scalpel safely sheebed,

575
00:23:26,160 --> 00:23:28,320
how do we step back out into the world?

576
00:23:28,320 --> 00:23:29,520
Having looked into the void

577
00:23:29,520 --> 00:23:31,840
and accepted that no value survives

578
00:23:31,840 --> 00:23:33,600
strict rational scrutiny,

579
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:36,160
the author's response is incredibly grounding.

580
00:23:36,960 --> 00:23:38,160
I came back to the human.

581
00:23:38,800 --> 00:23:40,400
Back to the messy, illogical,

582
00:23:40,400 --> 00:23:43,280
contingent attachments of an actual life,

583
00:23:43,280 --> 00:23:44,560
back to loving your family,

584
00:23:44,640 --> 00:23:45,920
caring about your friends,

585
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:47,200
crying at movies,

586
00:23:47,200 --> 00:23:50,480
but the entire context of why we do these things

587
00:23:50,480 --> 00:23:52,080
has fundamentally shifted.

588
00:23:52,080 --> 00:23:53,520
We aren't returning to these things

589
00:23:53,520 --> 00:23:56,640
because we suddenly found a hidden cosmic warrant for them.

590
00:23:56,640 --> 00:23:59,040
We already prove that doesn't exist.

591
00:23:59,040 --> 00:24:01,440
We return to them simply because we are human.

592
00:24:01,440 --> 00:24:03,440
There is no view from nowhere.

593
00:24:03,440 --> 00:24:04,880
We can't float in the void

594
00:24:04,880 --> 00:24:07,040
as pure nodes of disembodied logic.

595
00:24:07,040 --> 00:24:08,400
We are flesh, blood,

596
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:10,560
neurochemistry, and social needs.

597
00:24:10,560 --> 00:24:14,160
We are thrown in this specific biological and cultural form.

598
00:24:14,160 --> 00:24:17,600
The human experience is the only material we have to work with.

599
00:24:17,600 --> 00:24:20,000
So these connections and emotions become valuable,

600
00:24:20,000 --> 00:24:22,400
not because they are objective universal truths,

601
00:24:22,400 --> 00:24:24,560
but because they are the only ground available

602
00:24:24,560 --> 00:24:26,160
for a human to stand on.

603
00:24:26,160 --> 00:24:28,480
To really nail down the psychology of this return,

604
00:24:28,480 --> 00:24:30,480
the author tinds to Seren Kierkegaard,

605
00:24:30,480 --> 00:24:32,240
the 19th century Danish philosopher

606
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:34,480
and his concept of the Knight of Faith.

607
00:24:34,480 --> 00:24:38,000
Kierkegaard describes a profound double movement.

608
00:24:38,720 --> 00:24:42,240
The first movement is infinite resignation,

609
00:24:42,320 --> 00:24:44,400
which is everything we did with the scalpel,

610
00:24:44,400 --> 00:24:46,640
surrendering our demand for cosmic meaning,

611
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:48,160
accepting the void,

612
00:24:48,160 --> 00:24:51,840
resigning ourselves to the fact that the universe owes us absolutely nothing.

613
00:24:51,840 --> 00:24:53,520
A lot of philosophers stop there.

614
00:24:53,520 --> 00:24:56,000
They become knights of infinite resignation,

615
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:57,920
detached, cynical,

616
00:24:57,920 --> 00:24:59,440
hovering above the world.

617
00:24:59,440 --> 00:25:02,480
But the Knight of Faith makes the crucial second movement,

618
00:25:03,520 --> 00:25:06,880
a total passionate return to the finite world.

619
00:25:06,880 --> 00:25:10,240
The Knight of Faith dives back into the messy reality of life

620
00:25:10,240 --> 00:25:13,120
and loves it completely as if it had absolute meaning

621
00:25:13,120 --> 00:25:16,320
while simultaneously knowing with perfect clarity that it does not.

622
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:18,800
It's a paradox. Total release,

623
00:25:18,800 --> 00:25:20,720
followed immediately by total engagement.

624
00:25:21,360 --> 00:25:24,240
Now, when I first read this, my immediate reaction was skepticism.

625
00:25:24,240 --> 00:25:26,720
Doesn't this just sound like an intellectual cop out?

626
00:25:26,720 --> 00:25:28,880
Aren't we just plugging ourselves back into the matrix

627
00:25:28,880 --> 00:25:32,320
because the cold, hard desert of the real world was too depressing?

628
00:25:32,320 --> 00:25:34,720
It feels like we're just choosing to be naive again.

629
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:36,960
It's the most common critique of existentialism.

630
00:25:36,960 --> 00:25:38,960
Yeah, how is pretending something matters

631
00:25:38,960 --> 00:25:40,800
when you know it does not naive?

632
00:25:40,800 --> 00:25:42,240
Because of the order of operations.

633
00:25:42,960 --> 00:25:44,480
If you never went through the dissolution,

634
00:25:44,480 --> 00:25:46,960
if you never picked up the scalpel and faced the void,

635
00:25:46,960 --> 00:25:49,200
then yes, blindly loving the world,

636
00:25:49,200 --> 00:25:51,120
just because your culture told you to,

637
00:25:51,120 --> 00:25:53,120
is naive sentimentalism.

638
00:25:53,120 --> 00:25:54,880
You're just a battery in the matrix.

639
00:25:55,520 --> 00:25:57,680
But if you have visited the void,

640
00:25:57,680 --> 00:25:59,280
the return is transformed.

641
00:25:59,920 --> 00:26:02,880
You are looking at the green scrolling code of the matrix.

642
00:26:02,880 --> 00:26:06,240
You fully recognize that the state you're eating is a simulation.

643
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:09,600
But instead of sitting in a cold, gray hovercraft,

644
00:26:09,600 --> 00:26:12,800
eating protein, gruel out of misplaced pride,

645
00:26:12,800 --> 00:26:15,120
you consciously choose to sit at the table,

646
00:26:15,120 --> 00:26:16,560
taste the simulated steak,

647
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:18,800
and enjoy the simulated sunset.

648
00:26:18,800 --> 00:26:20,720
Because you recognize that the simulation

649
00:26:20,720 --> 00:26:23,440
is the only reality your human form can inhabit.

650
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:25,360
As the author says, without the return,

651
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:26,640
the release would be sterile.

652
00:26:27,200 --> 00:26:28,400
The void is sterile.

653
00:26:28,400 --> 00:26:30,640
Returning after the release is a fully conscious,

654
00:26:30,640 --> 00:26:32,480
open-eyed act of creation.

655
00:26:32,480 --> 00:26:34,960
The power lies entirely in the choosing.

656
00:26:35,040 --> 00:26:38,640
And this act of choosing brings up a massive philosophical paradox.

657
00:26:39,520 --> 00:26:42,480
If we used rationality to dissolve our small,

658
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:44,400
culturally programmed ego,

659
00:26:44,400 --> 00:26:48,560
if we prove that our self is just an illusion of biology and environment,

660
00:26:48,560 --> 00:26:51,040
who exactly is the agent making this grand,

661
00:26:51,040 --> 00:26:53,600
existential choice to return to the human?

662
00:26:53,600 --> 00:26:56,160
Who is doing the choosing if the chooser doesn't exist?

663
00:26:56,160 --> 00:26:56,880
Exactly.

664
00:26:56,880 --> 00:27:00,800
The author answers this by diving deep into the Buddhist concept of Anata,

665
00:27:00,800 --> 00:27:02,000
or no self.

666
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,400
This concept is heavily misunderstood in the West.

667
00:27:05,120 --> 00:27:07,440
We tend to read Anata as a vacuum.

668
00:27:07,440 --> 00:27:11,280
We think if there is no eye, then there is zero agency.

669
00:27:11,280 --> 00:27:13,680
I must just be a passive leaf blowing in the wind

670
00:27:13,680 --> 00:27:15,280
with no ability to make choices.

671
00:27:15,280 --> 00:27:17,680
We equate selflessness with helplessness.

672
00:27:17,680 --> 00:27:20,480
But the author points out that the deeper Mahayana Buddhist reading

673
00:27:20,480 --> 00:27:22,000
is the exact reverse.

674
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:25,440
Desolving the illusion of the bounded self doesn't destroy agency,

675
00:27:25,440 --> 00:27:28,320
it gives birth to total unbounded agency.

676
00:27:28,320 --> 00:27:32,320
Let's visualize this because unbounded agency is a tough concept to grow.

677
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:34,720
Imagine a massive suburban landscape.

678
00:27:35,040 --> 00:27:37,840
Every house has a tiny walled-off backyard.

679
00:27:37,840 --> 00:27:39,040
Okay, I'm picturing it.

680
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:41,520
Your ego, your traditional sense of self,

681
00:27:41,520 --> 00:27:43,520
is that tiny, fenced-in yard.

682
00:27:44,160 --> 00:27:47,120
All of your daily desires, anxieties, and choices

683
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:51,120
are entirely focused on defending and enriching that little patch of grass.

684
00:27:51,760 --> 00:27:54,400
You want your yard to be better than the neighbors.

685
00:27:54,400 --> 00:27:57,440
You are terrified someone will throw trash over your fence.

686
00:27:57,440 --> 00:27:59,760
You spend your whole life patrolling the perimeter.

687
00:27:59,760 --> 00:28:01,760
That is the state of the bounded self.

688
00:28:01,760 --> 00:28:03,920
High anxiety, limited perspective.

689
00:28:03,920 --> 00:28:06,480
Now, imagine using the scalpel of rationality

690
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:09,040
to tear down every single fence in the neighborhood.

691
00:28:09,040 --> 00:28:10,400
You don't stop existing.

692
00:28:10,400 --> 00:28:12,240
Your yard doesn't vanish into a black hole.

693
00:28:12,800 --> 00:28:17,520
But suddenly, the artificial boundary between my yard and your yard is gone.

694
00:28:17,520 --> 00:28:19,760
You inhabit the entire landscape.

695
00:28:19,760 --> 00:28:21,360
When the fences come down,

696
00:28:21,360 --> 00:28:24,480
how does that fundamentally alter your desires and your choices?

697
00:28:24,480 --> 00:28:28,000
The anxious defensive wanting of the tiny ego evaporates.

698
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,240
It is replaced by the wanting of the whole.

699
00:28:30,240 --> 00:28:33,840
You no longer want to hoard fertilizer for your specific patch of grass

700
00:28:33,840 --> 00:28:35,600
while the rest of the neighborhood dies.

701
00:28:35,600 --> 00:28:39,200
You want the entire landscape to thrive because you are the landscape.

702
00:28:39,200 --> 00:28:42,000
When the illusion of the small self is seen through,

703
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:46,240
the agent that remains isn't constrained to one terrified perspective.

704
00:28:46,240 --> 00:28:48,160
You don't have zero agency.

705
00:28:48,160 --> 00:28:50,560
You have the boundless agency of the whole.

706
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:54,160
This connects directly to the pinnacle of Mahayana Buddhist practice.

707
00:28:54,960 --> 00:28:56,160
The Bodhisattva.

708
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:59,760
The Bodhisattva is a being who has achieved the ultimate release.

709
00:29:00,400 --> 00:29:03,280
They have seen through the illusion of the bound itself

710
00:29:03,280 --> 00:29:05,440
and realized the void of emptiness.

711
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:09,120
But instead of checking out of reality and dissolving into Nirvana,

712
00:29:09,120 --> 00:29:13,920
they make a staggering vow to remain in the messy cycle of human existence

713
00:29:13,920 --> 00:29:17,680
until every single sentient being is liberated.

714
00:29:17,680 --> 00:29:18,880
Why on earth would they do that?

715
00:29:18,880 --> 00:29:21,680
If they know it's all an illusion, why stay in the burning house?

716
00:29:21,680 --> 00:29:23,280
Because there are no fences.

717
00:29:23,280 --> 00:29:24,960
They act for the benefit of all beings,

718
00:29:24,960 --> 00:29:29,200
precisely because the boundary between this being and other beings has been erased.

719
00:29:29,280 --> 00:29:31,840
The return to the human marketplace isn't a retreat.

720
00:29:31,840 --> 00:29:32,640
The author says,

721
00:29:32,640 --> 00:29:36,400
I do not retreat back into my small self because the void was too cold.

722
00:29:36,400 --> 00:29:39,680
The return is simply acting the way unbounded being acts

723
00:29:39,680 --> 00:29:42,240
when it is poured into a specific human form.

724
00:29:42,240 --> 00:29:44,880
You care for your family, you fight for justice,

725
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:48,640
you create art not out of a desperate ego-driven need

726
00:29:48,640 --> 00:29:50,880
to justify your own existence.

727
00:29:50,880 --> 00:29:55,520
But because care and creation are simply what a human form does

728
00:29:55,520 --> 00:29:58,000
when it is free from the anxiety of the ego.

729
00:29:58,080 --> 00:30:01,600
It is a radical, profound acceptance of our humanity.

730
00:30:02,320 --> 00:30:04,240
Arriving back at the starting line,

731
00:30:04,240 --> 00:30:05,840
not through naive ignorance,

732
00:30:05,840 --> 00:30:08,080
but by walking all the way through the void

733
00:30:08,080 --> 00:30:09,440
and coming out the other side.

734
00:30:09,440 --> 00:30:11,200
We've covered an immense amount of ground today.

735
00:30:11,200 --> 00:30:12,880
Let's pull this massive journey together.

736
00:30:12,880 --> 00:30:14,960
The author has systematically demonstrated

737
00:30:14,960 --> 00:30:17,920
that shouting into the night sky is anything honorable.

738
00:30:17,920 --> 00:30:19,520
What is the meaning of my life?

739
00:30:19,520 --> 00:30:21,280
Is fundamentally the wrong question?

740
00:30:21,280 --> 00:30:23,680
The cosmos will always answer that question

741
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:25,520
with absolute chilling silence.

742
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,960
There is no inherent objective honor

743
00:30:28,960 --> 00:30:32,160
in humanism, in ambition, or even in love.

744
00:30:32,160 --> 00:30:34,320
Pretending there's some cosmic safety net

745
00:30:34,320 --> 00:30:35,920
is intellectual dishonesty.

746
00:30:35,920 --> 00:30:38,000
The correct question, the only question left standing

747
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:39,840
after this gable has done its work is

748
00:30:39,840 --> 00:30:41,680
do I choose to honor anything?

749
00:30:41,680 --> 00:30:45,680
And because you are an organism thrown into a human form,

750
00:30:45,680 --> 00:30:47,840
the answer is almost certainly going to be yes,

751
00:30:48,800 --> 00:30:50,720
which leads to the ultimate realization.

752
00:30:51,760 --> 00:30:53,520
What do I choose to honor,

753
00:30:53,520 --> 00:30:55,120
knowing with absolute certainty

754
00:30:55,120 --> 00:30:57,360
that the honoring is the work of my own hands

755
00:30:57,360 --> 00:31:00,080
and not a verdict handed down by the stars?

756
00:31:00,080 --> 00:31:02,160
That shift from discovering meaning

757
00:31:02,160 --> 00:31:05,520
to consciously creating it is the ultimate empowerment.

758
00:31:05,520 --> 00:31:09,040
True agency doesn't belong to society's expectations.

759
00:31:09,040 --> 00:31:11,520
It doesn't belong to a rigid cosmic order.

760
00:31:11,520 --> 00:31:13,680
It belongs exclusively to your own hands

761
00:31:13,680 --> 00:31:16,320
as you extend them back into the human marketplace.

762
00:31:16,320 --> 00:31:19,760
You choose to care, fully aware that there is no underlying why.

763
00:31:20,400 --> 00:31:22,560
The author closes this profound exploration

764
00:31:22,640 --> 00:31:24,640
by returning to a line from the diamond sutra.

765
00:31:25,680 --> 00:31:28,480
Let the mind arise without dwelling anywhere.

766
00:31:28,480 --> 00:31:30,800
Attached to nothing but present everywhere.

767
00:31:30,800 --> 00:31:33,760
You don't dwell in the rigid concrete of dogmatic belief,

768
00:31:33,760 --> 00:31:36,480
demanding that the universe validate your choices,

769
00:31:36,480 --> 00:31:40,400
but you also don't dwell in the paralyzing cynical cold of the void.

770
00:31:40,400 --> 00:31:42,240
You let your mind arise freely,

771
00:31:42,240 --> 00:31:44,720
engaging with the world exactly where you are needed.

772
00:31:44,720 --> 00:31:46,000
You see the illusion,

773
00:31:46,000 --> 00:31:48,400
but your chop wood and carry water anyway,

774
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:50,160
with more care than ever before.

775
00:31:50,160 --> 00:31:53,760
We open this deep dive by talking about foundations.

776
00:31:53,760 --> 00:31:55,360
We want concrete.

777
00:31:55,360 --> 00:31:58,400
We want unquestionable bedrock to build our lives on.

778
00:31:58,400 --> 00:32:00,720
But what this essay forces us to realize

779
00:32:00,720 --> 00:32:03,520
is that building on the void isn't a tragedy.

780
00:32:03,520 --> 00:32:06,640
It is the absolute pinnacle of creative freedom.

781
00:32:06,640 --> 00:32:09,120
You do not need the universe's permission

782
00:32:09,120 --> 00:32:11,440
to build a beautiful, meaningful life.

783
00:32:11,440 --> 00:32:13,920
The void is simply the cleared field

784
00:32:13,920 --> 00:32:15,920
where wisdom finally gets the space

785
00:32:15,920 --> 00:32:17,680
to plant something entirely new.

786
00:32:17,760 --> 00:32:20,080
As we wrap up, I want to leave you the listener

787
00:32:20,080 --> 00:32:22,800
with a scenario to turn over in your mind.

788
00:32:22,800 --> 00:32:24,880
Imagine waking up tomorrow morning

789
00:32:24,880 --> 00:32:27,440
and in that brief moment before you get out of bed,

790
00:32:27,440 --> 00:32:31,600
you are fully, completely convinced of the reality we just explored.

791
00:32:31,600 --> 00:32:33,840
The universe expects absolutely nothing of you.

792
00:32:33,840 --> 00:32:35,200
There is no grand scoreboard.

793
00:32:35,200 --> 00:32:37,920
Every social pressure, every cultural expectation,

794
00:32:37,920 --> 00:32:39,840
every cosmic rule is a fiction.

795
00:32:39,840 --> 00:32:41,680
You are standing in the cleared field

796
00:32:41,680 --> 00:32:43,440
and the scalpel has done its work.

797
00:32:43,440 --> 00:32:45,520
You have total unbounded freedom.

798
00:32:45,520 --> 00:32:48,000
In that moment of absolute release,

799
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:50,560
what is the very first thing you would consciously choose

800
00:32:50,560 --> 00:32:51,760
to care about anyway?

801
00:32:51,760 --> 00:32:53,200
What is the first thing you would reach out

802
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:54,800
and touch in the marketplace?

803
00:32:54,800 --> 00:32:57,440
Because whatever you choose in that moment,

804
00:32:58,640 --> 00:33:01,360
that is where your actual life begins.

805
00:33:01,360 --> 00:33:04,320
Thank you for joining us on this incredibly intense journey.

806
00:33:04,320 --> 00:33:06,880
Keep questioning the map, keep choosing your essence

807
00:33:06,880 --> 00:33:08,960
and most importantly, enjoy the messy,

808
00:33:08,960 --> 00:33:11,600
beautiful, unwarranted reality of being human.

